+---
+
+## Dependencies & Execution Order
+
+### Phase Dependencies
+- Phase 1 (Setup): No dependencies; must finish before Phase 2.
+- Phase 2 (Foundational): Depends on Phase 1; blocks all user stories.
+- Phase 3 (US1 P1): Depends on Phase 2; delivers MVP.
+- Phase 4 (US2 P2): Depends on Phase 2; can run after or in parallel with Phase 3 once shared components stable.
+- Phase 5 (US3 P2): Depends on Phase 2 and RS485/session features from Phase 3.
+- Phase 6 (US4 P3): Depends on RS485 transport and session infrastructure (Phase 2 + Phase 3).
+- Phase 7 (Polish): Depends on completion of targeted user stories.
+
+### User Story Dependencies
+- **US1**: Requires foundational transport/storage (Phase 2). No other story dependencies.
+- **US2**: Requires segment mapping from Phase 2 and session context from US1.
+- **US3**: Requires session bus and LED mapping from US1/US2.
+- **US4**: Requires session bus and transport from US1; independent of US2/US3 logic.
+
+### Within-Story Order
+- Implement controllers/services before HTTP handlers for each story.
+- Persist configuration before exposing UI toggles dependent on that data.
+- Animation assets (T030) can build in parallel once controller scaffolding exists.
+
+## Parallel Execution Examples
+- **US1**: T014 (UI asset generation) can run in parallel with T013 (Arduino state machine) because they touch separate projects.
+- **US2**: T024 (diagnostic script) can run alongside T021 (LED rendering) to validate output concurrently.
+- **US3**: T030 (animation presets) can progress while T027 (event consumer) integrates RS485 feed.
+- **Cross-story**: Once Phase 2 complete, teams can split US1 focus on Arduino vs. ESP32 components concurrently using [P] flagged tasks.
+
+## Implementation Strategy
+
+### MVP First
+1. Complete Phase 1 and Phase 2 to establish transport and configuration.
+2. Deliver Phase 3 (US1) — minimal playable setup flow (MVP).
+3. Validate using quickstart checklist updates (T019).
+
+### Incremental Delivery
+1. After MVP, add seat visualization (Phase 4) to improve clarity.
+2. Introduce real-time feedback (Phase 5) for full gameplay experience.
+3. Finish with OTA management (Phase 6) and polish (Phase 7).
+
+### Parallel Team Allocation
+- Firmware dev A: Arduino-focused tasks (T013, T021, T026, T034).
+- Firmware dev B: ESP32 UI/API (T014, T016, T022, T029, T035).
+- Tools dev: Shared scripts and diagnostics (T007, T024, T031, T038).
